course: biology
lesson: 142
title: Zootopia: real sloths, shrews, predators and prey
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In Zootopia, Flash the sloth stamps papers so slowly that a whole queue waits, and Mr Big, a tiny shrew, rules a gang of polar bears.
explain: Real sloths are slow for a good reason: their leafy food gives very little energy, so they save every bit by moving slowly, keeping a low body temperature and hanging from hooked claws instead of holding themselves up.
explain: Real shrews are the opposite: tiny bodies lose heat fast, so a shrew must hunt and eat almost all the time, fierce as a small engine running flat out.
explain: Think of a nature film at a waterhole: hundreds of zebras and only a few lions, all drinking while the lions are full. Real predators and prey do share places, but predators hunt to eat, not because they «go savage», and there are always far more prey than hunters.
fact: Sloths eat leaves that give little energy, so they move slowly, have small muscles and a low body temperature, and can take a whole day to digest a meal.
fact: Long curved claws hook over branches, so a sloth can hang upside down for hours, even asleep, without tiring.
fact: Sloths are good swimmers and swim faster than they move on land; a sloth can hold its breath for up to about 40 minutes.
fact: Green algae grow in sloth fur and give camouflage among leaves, and moths live in the fur too.
fact: A sloth climbs down about once a week to go to the toilet on the ground; scientists are not sure why, and it is the most dangerous time of its week.
fact: Shrews are tiny fierce hunters of insects and worms; their small bodies lose heat quickly, so some eat their own weight in food every day, and a shrew can die if it goes a day without food.
fact: The smallest shrews weigh about as much as a paperclip or two, while an elephant weighs several tonnes, millions of times more.
fact: Many predators, such as cats and owls, have eyes in front to judge distance; many prey, such as rabbits and deer, have eyes on the sides to see all around.
fact: In nature there are always far more prey than predators, because each predator needs to eat many prey animals: the Serengeti has millions of wildebeest but only a few thousand lions.
fact: Predators and prey sometimes drink near each other, and zebras watch lions closely but run only if the lions come towards them.
fact: Real predators hunt to eat, not out of anger or meanness, and many fail more hunts than they win.
exercise: Why is a real sloth so slow? | Its leafy food gives very little energy, so it saves energy | It is lazy and simply does not want to work, so it hangs about all day | Its legs are broken when it is young | It is always half asleep from eating fruit
exercise: How can a sloth hang upside down for hours without tiring? | Its curved claws hook over the branch | It glues itself to the bark with sticky fur | It holds on with its strong tail like a monkey | It takes turns with each foot every minute
exercise: Can a sloth swim? | Yes, it swims faster than it moves on land | No, sloths sink straight away | Only baby sloths can swim | Only in water warmer than a bath, because cold water stops its heart
exercise: Why does a sloth's fur look greenish? | Green algae grow in it, hiding the sloth among leaves | The sloth rubs green leaves into its fur every morning to dye it the colour of the trees | Sloth hair is green from the moment it grows | The sloth is ill and its skin turns green
exercise: Why does a sloth climb down a tree about once a week? | To go to the toilet | To look for a new mate every week | To drink from a river | To wash its fur in the mud
exercise: Why is the weekly trip down the most dangerous time for a sloth? | Predators can catch it on the ground | The ground is too hot for its feet | It might forget the way back up | Other sloths fight it on the ground
exercise: Why must a real shrew eat almost all the time? | Its tiny body loses heat very fast | It has no stomach to store food | It is always growing bigger | It shares its food with its family
exercise: How much can some shrews eat in a day? | About their own body weight | About one tiny crumb of bread | About as much as a grown rabbit | About a hundred times their weight
exercise: About how much does the smallest shrew weigh? | About as much as a paperclip or two | About as much as an apple | About as much as a cat | About as much as a school bag
exercise: Where are a hunter's eyes, such as a cat's or an owl's? | In front, to judge how far away prey is | On the sides, to see all around | On top, to look up at the sky | One in front and one at the back
exercise: Why do rabbits and deer have eyes on the sides of their heads? | To see danger coming from almost all around | To see far better in the dark | To judge distance for pouncing | To look at two foods at once
exercise: In a real savanna, which is true about predators and prey? | There are far more prey than predators | There are far more predators than prey | There are equal numbers of both | There are no prey left at all
exercise: Lions and zebras drink at the same waterhole. When does a zebra usually run? | When the lions start coming towards it | Whenever it sees any lion at all | Only after a lion has bitten it | Never, zebras are not scared of lions
exercise: Why do real predators hunt? | To eat and survive | Because they are angry and mean | To show off to other predators | Because they hate the prey
exercise: In Zootopia predators «go savage». What is true of real hunts? | Many predators fail more hunts than they win | Predators win every single hunt | Predators hunt only when angry | Predators hunt prey they do not eat
test: «3 sloths hanging in a tree, how many legs hooked on the branch if they all use all 4» | 12 | 7 | 3 | 16
test: «Flash is slow coz sloths are just lazy, simple» What is right? | Sloths save energy because their leafy food gives very little | Yes, sloths could easily move fast if they wanted, but they choose not to bother | Yes, sloths sleep because they are bored | No, sloths are slow because they are old
test: In a cartoon, a sloth falls in a river and sinks like a stone. What is real? | Sloths swim well and can hold their breath for a long time | Sloths sink straight to the bottom because their long fur soaks up so much water | Sloths cannot float at all | Sloths drown in a few seconds
test: A sloth stayed hidden in the treetops for months, then got caught on the ground. Why there? | It climbs down about once a week for the toilet, and that is when it is in danger | It came down to look for a new tree to sleep in, as sloths change trees every night | It fell because sloths cannot grip well | It came down to hunt mice
test: What would happen if a shrew went a whole day without eating? | It could die, because its tiny body burns food so fast | It would be fine for a few weeks | It would grow bigger and slower, saving up fat from the food it had already eaten | It would hibernate till spring
test: «Mr Big's a crime boss coz shrews are scary tough, lol» What is true about real shrews? | They are fierce little hunters of insects and worms | They are gentle little animals that eat only seeds and never hunt any other creature | They hunt polar bears in the Arctic | They are scared of every insect
test: A mouse and an elephant walk down the same street in the film. Roughly how many times heavier is a big elephant than the smallest shrew? | Millions of times | About ten times heavier | About a hundred times | About twice as heavy
test: «shut up, a lion's not scared of anything so why's it got eyes in front?» Why does a hunter have front eyes? | To judge distance when it pounces | To look fierce and scary to other lions | To see behind itself while running | To keep the blowing sand out of its eyes
test: A wildlife film shows one lion pride and a huge herd of zebras. Why is that the usual picture? | Each predator needs many prey animals to survive | Lions have scared off most other lions | Zebras eat lions in bad years | Lions only ever live in very small groups by chance, not because of how much food there is
test: «predators go savage when they're angry, like in Zootopia» What is right? | Real predators hunt to eat, not out of anger | Yes, real lions attack other animals mainly when they are cross or in a bad mood | Yes, wolves hunt to punish other animals | No, real predators never hunt at all
test: «a sloth hanging in a rainforest, 6 moths living in its fur, how many animals is that altogether» | 7 | 6 | 1 | 12
exam: Identify the principal reason for the exceptionally slow movement of the sloth. | Its diet of foliage furnishes very little energy, which it must therefore conserve | It is constitutionally disinclined to exertion | Its limbs are incapable of rapid motion owing to injury | It is perpetually drowsy from the consumption of fermented fruit, which it eats in large amounts
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the dietary requirements of the shrew. | Owing to rapid loss of heat, certain shrews consume their own body weight in food daily | The shrew requires nourishment no more than once a week, having stored fat beneath its skin for the purpose | The shrew subsists entirely upon seeds and grain | The shrew obtains its food by preying upon large mammals
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the position of the eyes in predator and prey? | Predators commonly possess forward-facing eyes for judging distance; prey commonly possess lateral eyes for a wide view | Prey commonly possess forward-facing eyes for judging distance | Predators commonly possess lateral eyes for a wide view | The position of the eyes bears no relation to the manner of life, being decided entirely by the size of the head
exam: Ascertain the relative abundance of predators and prey in a natural ecosystem such as the Serengeti. | Prey animals greatly outnumber predators | Predators greatly outnumber prey animals | Predators and prey occur in equal numbers | Prey animals are wholly absent
